<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <a href="/docs" class="inline-flex items-center text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 font-medium transition-colors group">
      <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2 transform group-hover:-translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
      </svg>
      Back to Documentation
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-12">
    <main class="lg:col-span-12 space-y-12">
      <div class="relative">
        <div class="absolute -inset-1 bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-500 to-teal-600 rounded-lg blur opacity-25"></div>
        <div class="relative">
          <h1 class="text-5xl font-extrabold text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-400 via-teal-400 to-cyan-400 mb-4 tracking-tight">
            Foreign Keys
          </h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
            Declare that a field holds the <code class="text-indigo-300">_key</code> of a document of another collection, and the database refuses a reference to a document that is not there.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Overview</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              A foreign key is declared on the collection that holds the reference (the <em>child</em>) and names a collection of the same database (the <em>parent</em>). From then on:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300">
              <li>a write that gives the field a value is refused with <code class="text-indigo-300">409</code> unless a parent document has that <code class="text-indigo-300">_key</code>. <code class="text-indigo-300">null</code> and an absent field refer to nothing and are always accepted;</li>
              <li>deleting a parent document follows the key's <code class="text-indigo-300">on_delete</code> rule: refused, or completed with its children, in the same atomic write;</li>
              <li>the parent collection cannot be dropped, nor truncated while the child holds documents.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              The constraint is enforced by storage, so it holds for every writer: the document API, SDBQL, the native driver, Lua scripts, imports and transactions. The field holds the parent's <code class="text-indigo-300">_key</code>, or its <code class="text-indigo-300">_id</code>, which is how an edge's <code class="text-indigo-300">_from</code> and <code class="text-indigo-300">_to</code> are covered (<a href="#edges" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300">below</a>). A collection with no foreign key, and that no key refers to, pays nothing for the feature.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="declare" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-indigo-500/10 to-blue-500/10 rounded-xl border border-indigo-500/20 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="p-5 border-b border-indigo-500/20 bg-indigo-500/5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-indigo-300">Declaring a key</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-lg border border-white/5 p-4">
              <pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">curl -X POST http://localhost:6745/_api/database/shop/collection/orders/foreign-keys \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"field": "customer", "references": "customers", "on_delete": "cascade"}'

{"name": "fk_customer", "field": "customer", "references": "customers",
 "on_delete": "cascade", "index": "fk_customer"}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-gray-400">
                <thead class="text-xs uppercase text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10">
                  <tr><th class="py-2 pr-4">Field</th><th class="py-2">Meaning</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">field</code></td><td class="py-2">A top-level field of the child that is not a system attribute. Its value is the parent's <code class="text-indigo-300">_key</code> (a string), or <code class="text-indigo-300">null</code>.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">references</code></td><td class="py-2">The parent collection, in the same database. It may be the child itself (a tree).</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">on_delete</code></td><td class="py-2"><code class="text-indigo-300">restrict</code> (the default), <code class="text-indigo-300">cascade</code> or <code class="text-indigo-300">set_null</code>.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">by</code></td><td class="py-2"><code class="text-indigo-300">key</code> (the default) or <code class="text-indigo-300">id</code>: whether the field holds the parent's <code class="text-indigo-300">_key</code> or its <code class="text-indigo-300">_id</code>.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">name</code></td><td class="py-2">Optional; <code class="text-indigo-300">fk_&lt;field&gt;</code> by default. One key per field.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              Children are found through an index on the field. A persistent or hash index on that single field is used when there is one; otherwise a persistent index named <code class="text-indigo-300">fk_&lt;field&gt;</code> is created. That index cannot be dropped while the key exists.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              Documents already stored are checked. If some refer to a missing parent the declaration is refused with <code class="text-indigo-300">409</code>, the first offending keys in the message, and nothing is kept. To see them without declaring anything:
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-lg border border-white/5 p-4">
              <pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">curl -X POST .../collection/orders/foreign-keys/check \
  -d '{"field": "customer", "references": "customers"}'

{"count": 1, "sample": [{"_key": "o7", "customer": "nobody"}]}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              <code class="text-indigo-300">GET .../collection/{name}/foreign-keys</code> lists a collection's keys (<code class="text-indigo-300">foreign_keys</code>) and the keys of other collections that refer to it (<code class="text-indigo-300">referenced_by</code>). <code class="text-indigo-300">DELETE .../foreign-keys/{name}</code> removes a key and leaves its index.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              The native driver has the same four operations (<code class="text-indigo-300">list_foreign_keys</code>, <code class="text-indigo-300">create_foreign_key</code>, <code class="text-indigo-300">delete_foreign_key</code>, <code class="text-indigo-300">check_foreign_key</code>), which the Rust client exposes under those names. The admin console shows and edits a collection's keys in its index panel.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="edges" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-500/10 to-teal-500/10 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="p-5 border-b border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-300">Edges, and references by <code>_id</code></h3>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              With <code class="text-indigo-300">"by": "id"</code> the field holds the parent's <code class="text-indigo-300">_id</code>, with or without its database: <code class="text-indigo-300">customers/ann</code> and <code class="text-indigo-300">shop:customers/ann</code> name the same document. A value that names another collection or another database is refused.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              That is the form of an edge's ends, and the default for them: on an edge collection, <code class="text-indigo-300">_from</code> and <code class="text-indigo-300">_to</code> can each carry a key, served by the indexes every edge collection has.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-lg border border-white/5 p-4">
              <pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">curl -X POST .../collection/follows/foreign-keys \
  -d '{"field": "_from", "references": "customers", "on_delete": "cascade"}'

{"name": "fk_from", "field": "_from", "by": "id", "references": "customers",
 "on_delete": "cascade", "index": "_edge_from_idx"}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              An edge whose end names a vertex that is not there is then refused, and deleting a vertex deletes its edges (<code class="text-indigo-300">cascade</code>) or is refused while it has some (<code class="text-indigo-300">restrict</code>). <code class="text-indigo-300">set_null</code> is not offered for an edge's ends: an edge without one is not an edge. A key names one collection, so an edge collection that links vertices of several collections cannot declare one on that end.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="on-delete" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-500/10 to-orange-500/10 rounded-xl border border-amber-500/20 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="p-5 border-b border-amber-500/20 bg-amber-500/5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-300">Deleting a parent</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-gray-400">
                <thead class="text-xs uppercase text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10">
                  <tr><th class="py-2 pr-4"><code>on_delete</code></th><th class="py-2">When a parent with children is deleted</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">restrict</code></td><td class="py-2">The delete is refused with <code class="text-indigo-300">409</code>. A batch delete is one write: none of its documents is deleted.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">cascade</code></td><td class="py-2">The children are deleted with it, and their own children in turn, following each key's rule.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code class="text-indigo-300">set_null</code></td><td class="py-2">The children stay, with the field set to <code class="text-indigo-300">null</code> and a new <code class="text-indigo-300">_rev</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              The parent's delete and everything it causes are one write batch: all of it is stored or none of it, index entries included. Children deleted or updated by a cascade are announced on their collection's changefeed like any other write. A cascade that would write more than 100,000 documents (<code class="text-indigo-300">SOLIDB_FK_MAX_CASCADE</code>) is refused.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              A document expired by a TTL index is deleted under the same rule: under <code class="text-indigo-300">restrict</code> it stays until its children are gone.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="transactions" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">In a transaction</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              A transaction is checked at commit, as a whole: a child may be staged before the parent it refers to, and a parent may be deleted together with its children, in any order. A commit that would leave a child without its parent is refused with <code class="text-indigo-300">409</code> and writes nothing. A delete staged in a transaction cascades at commit; a child the same transaction wrote and that still refers to the deleted parent refuses the commit instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="replication" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Replication, dumps and restores</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-white">Replication.</strong> A key's definition is replicated like an index's. The node that receives a write checks it; the other nodes apply it as it was written, without checking it again, because entries can arrive out of order (a child before its parent). A node that joins a cluster receives the keys with the collections, in its full sync. The documents a delete cascades into are replicated as writes of their own, after the delete, and reach <a href="/docs/offline-sync" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300">offline-sync</a> clients the same way. Two nodes that accept conflicting writes at the same moment (one inserts a child, the other deletes its parent) can still end with an orphan: the check endpoint finds them, as <code class="text-indigo-300">POST /_api/database/{db}/index/{collection}/check</code> finds a unique value two nodes each accepted.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-white">Dump and restore.</strong> <code class="text-indigo-300">solidb-dump</code> writes each key with its collection. <code class="text-indigo-300">solidb-restore</code> declares the keys once every document is in, so the order of the collections in a dump does not matter; a key the restored data breaks is reported as a failed item and left out. With <code class="text-indigo-300">--drop</code>, the keys that refer to a dropped collection are set aside and declared again at the end.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-white">Imports.</strong> An import into a collection that has a key is checked document by document: load the parents first.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Limits</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-4">
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300">
              <li><strong class="text-white">No sharded collections.</strong> A key cannot be declared on a sharded collection, and a collection that takes part in a key cannot be sharded: a reference is checked against the documents of one node.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Document and edge collections</strong> of one database; no system collection. A key names one parent collection.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Deletes in a referenced collection are exclusive.</strong> While one runs, writes to the collections of that database that take part in a foreign key wait. Inserts and updates run concurrently as before.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Declare on quiet collections.</strong> A write to the child that was already running when the key was declared is not checked; run the check endpoint afterwards if writers were active.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</div>
